  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This book shows clear signs of public visibility and active use. It has been mentioned in Wikipedia-derived attention signals, is available in open access form through a hybrid model, and has recorded 1,620 OAPEN downloads, indicating sustained readership beyond the publisher record. The evidence points to a work that is circulating in public knowledge environments as well as in scholarly and library contexts.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Fake news in oorlogstijd is a 2021 scholarly book by Bruno Yammine, published by Leuven University Press, that examines propaganda and conflict in occupied Belgium during the First World War. The record places it in the humanities, with clear resonance for questions of peace, justice, and institutions. Funding support from KU Leuven and the Fondation Universitaire de Belgique is documented, and the book is available in open access alongside a broader hybrid distribution.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest impact signals are public-facing rather than citation-based. The book has 9 attention mentions associated with Wikipedia, which suggests it is being used or referenced in public knowledge contexts. That kind of visibility matters for a historical and political topic such as wartime propaganda, because it indicates that the work is entering wider informational ecosystems, not only scholarly ones.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Usage evidence also points to ongoing readership. OAPEN records 1,620 downloads, with activity spread across the year rather than confined to a single moment. In other words, the book is being accessed as a living resource. Because downloads and attention are different from citations, they should be read as signs of reach and engagement rather than scholarly uptake.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citation record is still early. OpenAlex currently shows 0 citations, so there is not yet a citation base strong enough to support claims about scholarly influence or field standing. The absence of citation growth does not outweigh the stronger signals of access and public visibility; it simply means that the measurable scholarly reception has not yet accumulated in the sources available here.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Overall, the record portrays a book with meaningful public and educational relevance, especially given its open availability and its alignment with themes of peace, justice, and institutions. The available evidence supports a narrative of reach, accessibility, and topical significance, even though formal citation-based impact remains limited at this stage.</p>",
